<p>China has built the world's largest and most advanced railway network — over 45,000 kilometers of high-speed rail alone, more than the rest of the world combined. For Silk Road travelers, trains aren't just transportation; they're a core part of the experience. Here's our complete guide to navigating China's trains, from bullet trains on the plains to overnight sleepers across the Gobi.</p>

<h2>The Two Systems: High-Speed vs. Conventional</h2>
<p>China's rail system splits into two categories. <strong>G-trains and D-trains</strong> (high-speed) operate on dedicated tracks at 250-350 km/h, connecting major cities. A G-train from Xi'an to Lanzhou takes just 3 hours — the same journey once took 12 hours. <strong>K-trains, T-trains, and Z-trains</strong> are conventional services — slower but atmospheric, with sleeper cabins where you can watch the landscape change as you cross from green valleys into desert. For Silk Road travelers, mixing both types creates the most authentic journey.</p>

